trace.content:
[THOUGHT]  
The clinical summary presents a classic immunophenotypic and clinical profile of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), now widely recognized as the leukemic phase of small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL)—a single disease spectrum per WHO and ICC classifications. Key diagnostic anchors include:  
- Sustained absolute lymphocytosis >5 × 10⁹/L for >3 months, excluding reactive causes (e.g., viral infection, autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome);  
- Flow cytometry findings highly specific for CLL/SLL: CD5+/CD23+/CD43+, dim CD20 and CD79b, FMC7−, and dim surface Ig — this pattern distinguishes it from mantle cell lymphoma (FMC7+, cyclin D1+), marginal zone lymphoma (CD5−), and follicular lymphoma (CD10+, BCL2+ in germinal center context);  
- Symptom burden (fatigue, malaise, >10% weight loss in 6 months) meets iwCLL criteria for *symptomatic disease*, even in absence of cytopenias or bulky lymphadenopathy — such systemic symptoms (“B symptoms”) are considered treatment indications when attributable to CLL and progressive;  
- Mild cytopenias raise concern for early bone marrow infiltration or immune-mediated cytopenias (e.g., AIHA, ITP), both common in CLL and potentially treatment-triggering;  
- Rai Stage 0–I is provisional but consistent with absence of organomegaly or major cytopenias *at this time* — however, the presence of significant B symptoms and weight loss may upstage to Rai II–III if exam/imaging reveals splenomegaly or lymphadenopathy, or if cytopenias worsen;  
- All recommended next steps (FISH, TP53, IGHV, BM biopsy, imaging) align with iwCLL risk stratification and treatment decision frameworks — notably, del(17p)/TP53 mutation or unmutated IGHV portend inferior response to chemoimmunotherapy and favor BTK inhibitor–based approaches.  

Importantly, no features suggest an alternative diagnosis: no plasmacytoid morphology, no CD10 or BCL6 expression (argues against FL), no t(11;14) or SOX11 (argues against MCL), no monoclonal gammopathy or lytic lesions (argues against multiple myeloma), and no extranodal mucosal involvement or H. pylori association (argues against MALT lymphoma). The constellation is highly specific.

[DIAGNOSIS]  
Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL), symptomatic, Rai Stage 0–I (pending staging evaluation), high-risk features pending molecular testing.

